## Proof

We determine the operator norm $\|A\| = \sup_{\|f\|_{L^1[1,2]} \leq 1} \|Af\|_{L^1[0,1]}$.

**Upper bound.** For $f \in L^1[1,2]$ with $\|f\|_1 \leq 1$,

$$\|Af\|_{L^1[0,1]} = \int_0^1 \left|\int_1^2 \frac{f(t)}{x+t}\,dt\right| dx \leq \int_0^1 \int_1^2 \frac{|f(t)|}{x+t}\,dt\,dx.$$

Since the integrand is non-negative, Tonelli's theorem gives

$$\|Af\|_{L^1[0,1]} \leq \int_1^2 |f(t)| \left(\int_0^1 \frac{dx}{x+t}\right) dt = \int_1^2 |f(t)| \ln\!\left(\frac{1+t}{t}\right) dt.$$

Define $g(t) = \ln\frac{1+t}{t} = \ln(1+t) - \ln t$ on $[1,2]$. Since

$$g'(t) = \frac{1}{1+t} - \frac{1}{t} = \frac{-1}{t(1+t)} < 0,$$

$g$ is strictly decreasing, so its maximum on $[1,2]$ is $g(1) = \ln 2$. Therefore

$$\|Af\|_{L^1[0,1]} \leq \ln 2 \int_1^2 |f(t)|\,dt = \ln 2 \cdot \|f\|_1 \leq \ln 2.$$

This gives $\|A\| \leq \ln 2$.

**Lower bound.** We show the bound is attained in the limit. For $n \geq 1$, set

$$f_n(t) = n\,\mathbf{1}_{[1,\,1+1/n]}(t),$$

so that $\|f_n\|_{L^1[1,2]} = 1$. Since $f_n \geq 0$, $Af_n \geq 0$ and

$$\|Af_n\|_{L^1[0,1]} = \int_0^1 \int_1^{1+1/n} \frac{n}{x+t}\,dt\,dx = n\int_1^{1+1/n} \ln\!\left(\frac{1+t}{t}\right) dt.$$

By the mean value theorem for integrals (or continuity of $g$),

$$n\int_1^{1+1/n} g(t)\,dt \xrightarrow{n\to\infty} g(1) = \ln 2.$$

Hence $\|A\| \geq \lim_{n\to\infty} \|Af_n\|_{L^1[0,1]} = \ln 2$.

**Conclusion.** Combining both bounds,

$$\boxed{\|A\| = \ln 2.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
